#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553646} :activity/id #uuid "2d018b8f-2cb9-5071-bceb-06a7dbb334a1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050224 :topic/id #uuid "37969d71-54e0-5479-8db0-03f8bc2bcfc3" :topic/math-academy-id 4160} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c87aef5-ea40-593e-a9d5-aefa7bc0b47e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553066} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4143c5-4aaf-5240-a3bb-dd85ed36c046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553217} {:answer/id #uuid "cfad29ab-2014-585b-8cfe-0bd344b97623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553284} {:answer/id #uuid "ee223723-3c0a-530d-bdaa-ad54b0e416ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553338} {:answer/id #uuid "fd22e3e1-c3c5-5819-b13e-ae357d0d0b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553369} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0560d7-2411-5cb7-8285-f3b1b348a142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a74f654-b8c0-5b05-930c-8a74a2b8b3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162549" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b626ea7f1db9e9f7cf92441e3669cbca85a8d469515e5185e87d241440d1bde.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is *not* a source-free vector field. \n\nSince $F$ is not source-free, we do not have enough information to determine the flux of $F$ through $C.$"} :step/id #uuid "7efc6d5d-247b-5ec0-8dbf-afc93829600b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553679}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bd42f49-0ece-5ce9-8b34-6a707862e044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552949} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab526ed-224c-541f-a66d-92563c921b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552987} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccab4e0-07a0-5a0f-b2ca-1faf663c12d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553090} {:answer/id #uuid "7d16d138-325b-50ca-b473-15666930d48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{76}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553122} {:answer/id #uuid "95d56263-122e-5c18-988e-3b958f4009d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552987} :answer-field/id #uuid "441e4f24-d3a1-5f34-b559-dd35657f1f13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d222c5e4-f6d8-59cd-b06f-5764984195b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162651" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7c48912df96585f7af01f3d0c1af904d1ca554555f7c63147fb5e0aa6676884.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,3)$ and $B(5,3).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,i+(x-1)^{2}\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector  ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2})=0+0=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,3)$ to $B(5,3)$ and ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3d/3d64ffb0e4cc313a2857d1c20922c1202619d0bdeb3d97f2808b4995c99f7f9f.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+3\\,j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+3\\,j)-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}=-\\sqrt{(3)^{3}}=-\\sqrt{27}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =(x-1)^{2}=((4t+1)-1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\sqrt{27})\\cdot (0)-(16{t}^{2})\\cdot (4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-64{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{64{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{64}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-\\frac{64}{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "84ca85c7-8f02-50ac-8aa5-148160d0bb9b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ea153ff-ca9a-53b6-87e8-2bc6a0c2501b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372099} {:answer/id #uuid "84df548c-6bde-5a63-97d5-4fbf449c3966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372100} {:answer/id #uuid "a3202131-59f4-5fd9-9f90-b0703666599e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372101} {:answer/id #uuid "dfaf9c30-a918-58ab-9ce8-23b59599fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372102} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2c2c57-5714-5fbf-9c4e-fdaf923dacc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372103}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372103} :answer-field/id #uuid "b38ccff0-d5a9-53de-b4dc-3a512c813886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe6d87cd-8e17-54ec-91d3-1c49d7e4c19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162545" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1cac5607abe416bf2ba5b4a6af42315edcc898034331034de68dd4c9be151c5e.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,ds$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,ds={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,ds.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,ds=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8bc7bf0d-8a57-5ed4-902b-c6da640ff1d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553679 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "287d9505-ed14-5cee-90ec-50d38ff00887" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552939} {:answer/id #uuid "471f6853-2555-57e6-a1c4-91e6fe90085d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553015} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca52f83-c8fb-58ed-8eda-01bc85761a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553035} {:answer/id #uuid "6f421748-53a9-541f-b31e-840a1b3dc0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553094} {:answer/id #uuid "e924ac84-61a3-537f-b4fd-6ebe6af690e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552939} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d52351a-5e24-5caf-bde1-3eef0cbcd908" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f712531-9b84-53ea-bba8-2a30378e29b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162555" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0$\n\nII. There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F$\n\nIII. $F$ is a source-free vector field" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+2x=4x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Indeed, this follows from statement I.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ec68a3c6-ceae-5e0c-a804-66d3a7e4a8fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043a47af-d945-52f5-a2ff-8dbd6cbb4d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552873} {:answer/id #uuid "084f29ee-31a1-58ac-a67f-c03a521c322a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552877} {:answer/id #uuid "0c339ee0-0900-5543-a85c-daf2f6f91a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552881} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba0bf79-9f84-57c9-91e2-72a01fc879ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552916} {:answer/id #uuid "e589549d-f5f7-5a08-a2a2-44640623c1a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552873} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e1b0569-2ef4-5afb-bff6-cf96b04525ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "27c3df67-079e-5e01-9714-60648afd83ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162628" :question/problem "![](images/84/84cfc63faa3514c7b6151d5ab8f3f8e4e7deaafa8739fa637af2f0ce7453b7fb.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,1)$ and $B(5,4).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2xy)=2x-2x=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the straight line from $A(1,1)$ to $B(5,4)$ and  ${n}_{2}$ is its unit normal, shown in the diagram below. \n\n![](images/18/18b1a5bdc11ca3c2b4b728cf8df3a3e1c4b6c2fbbe86549e9ad773367495c533.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nParametrizing ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+j+t\\cdot [(5\\,i+4j)-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(4t+1)\\,i+(3t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in [0,1].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={x}^{2}=(4t+1)^{2}=16{t}^{2}+8t+1, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-2xy=-2(4t+1)(3t+1)=-24{t}^{2}-14t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+1)=4, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((16{t}^{2}+8t+1)\\cdot (3)-(-24{t}^{2}-14t-2)\\cdot (4))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\,144{t}^{2}+80t+11\\,)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[48{t}^{3}+40{t}^{2}+11t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(48+40+11) \\\\  & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=99.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7a4d50d-bcd8-51eb-8dbc-add455b212bf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553641}}] :activity/title "Source-Free Vector Fields" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552853}]}
